{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian newspaper has issued a corrections statement regarding several articles published on August 25th. These corrections address factual inaccuracies and clarifications needed in various reports. The statement aims to ensure the accuracy and precision of information presented to readers. The corrections span across different sections of the newspaper, including news, business, arts, and history. The paper emphasizes its commitment to journalistic integrity and promptly rectifying any errors that may occur. This demonstrates a dedication to responsible reporting and maintaining reader trust.\n\n***\n\nSection 2\nOne correction clarifies the scope of a new pet theft law. Initially reported as applying \"in the UK,\" the law specifically applies to England and Northern Ireland. Another correction concerns the identification of individuals in articles. Eileen Burbidge is accurately identified as the executive director of Fertifa, not the founder, with Tony Chen and Nick Kuan holding that title. Similarly, the actor Juliet Stevenson's recent collaboration was with Alex Lawther, not \"Lowther.\" The family depicted in the film *Bend It Like Beckham* are Sikh, not Muslim.\n\nSection 3\nFurther corrections address historical details. A previous article incorrectly stated that Venetia Stanley's father converted to Islam; it was her uncle who did so. The statement regarding Pavel Durov's arrest at a French airport is also included in the list of amended articles. Other headlines mentioned in the statement include stories about royal servants, the Pope's views on England's football team, Britain's potential as a sci-tech superpower, and the *Salvator Mundi* painting. The statement also notes articles on wine and historical figures.\n\nSection 4\nReaders are invited 